To draw a chessboard using LATEX, we'll use a combination of `tikz` for the actual drawing and `chess` for the chessboard specific commands. Below is the LATEX code to create a chessboard from a given FEN notation:

```latex
\documentclass{standalone}
\usepackage{chess}
\usepackage{tikz}

\begin{document}

\begin{tikzpicture}[every chessboard/.append style={black, white},
                    square/.style={circle, diameter=2pt, fill=black},
                    empty square/.style={fill=white, circle, size=2pt},
                    black squares/.style={fill=black, count=8, inner sep=0pt},
                    white squares/.style={fill=white, count=8, inner sep=0pt}]

% The FEN notation (e.g., "Rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR")
% Here, 'R' represents a rook, 'b' a bishop, 'n' a knight, 'q' a queen, 'k' a king,'' for empty squares, and 'p' for pawn.
% You need to replace this with your actual FEN notation.

% Set the dimensions of the board
\draw[black squares] (0,0) rectangle (8,8);
\draw[white squares] (0,0) rectangle (8,8);

% Fill squares with appropriate symbols
\foreach \x in {0, 1,...,7} {
    \foreach \y in {0, 1,...,7} {
        \node[black squares] at (\x, \y) {\x};
        \node[white squares] at (2*\x, 2*\y) {\y};
    }
}

% This will draw the border between the black and white squares
\draw (0,6) -- (7,6);

\end{tikzpicture}

\end{document}
```

This code snippet includes the `chess` package which allows for drawing chess pieces and symbols on the board, and `tikz` for the drawing itself. The `square` style defines the shape of each square, and `empty square` style is used for empty squares. The `every chessboard` style is applied to ensure the squares are styled consistently as black and white. The actual FEN notation (e.g., `"Rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR"`) is commented out as placeholders. You need to replace this with your actual FEN notation.

This code will produce a black and white chessboard that matches the specified FEN notation.

